---
description: >
  Brevity-sweep audit of `scripts/.aide` — close to caps but carries forbidden content (exact strings, file paths, marketplace/library names, exact metadata values) saturating outcomes and Strategy.
intent: >
  `scripts/.aide` describes the PDF export script for copyright registration.
  Frontmatter cap counts are close to the bar but content fails the
  forbidden-content scan: outcomes hardcode exact title strings, exact author
  identity, exact dates, exact file paths and library names. Strategy enumerates
  specific Node packages by name and CLI commands. Trim cleanly possible.
misalignment:
  - spec-bloat
---

## Issues

- [ ] `outcomes.desired[0]` hardcodes the exact title string ("The AIDE Methodology
  Specification v1.0"), the exact author name (Jacob Carpenter / TetsuKodai
  Group LLC), the exact copyright notice ("© 2026 TetsuKodai Group LLC. All
  rights reserved."), and the exact publication date (April 12, 2026). Per the
  Brevity Contract, "exact strings the implementation produces" are forbidden
  content. The legal-discrepancy concern is real but belongs in a code constants
  module, with a unit test asserting the rendered cover matches; the spec only
  needs to commit to "the cover page metadata matches the author's
  copyright-registration record verbatim."
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[0]` | Forbidden content (exact strings)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Strategy` enumerates specific Node packages by name (Puppeteer, marked,
  markdown-it, pdf-lib, jsPDF) and specific CLI flags (`page.pdf()`,
  `headerTemplate`, `footerTemplate`, `displayHeaderFooter`, `@page`). Per the
  contract, function/symbol names and library names are forbidden. The decision
  here is "use a markdown→HTML→PDF pipeline with a print-quality renderer";
  the specific tool choices belong in the plan or in code with rationale
  comments.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Forbidden content (function/symbol names, exact strings)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Strategy` enumerates exact paths and skill subdirectory names (`brain/`,
  `study-playbook/`, `.aide/docs/`, `.claude/agents/`, `.claude/commands/`,
  `.claude/skills/`, `dist/aide-methodology.pdf`). File paths and folder layouts
  are forbidden content.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Forbidden content (file paths)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 5 items (cap 4). Item 4 ("running footer with
  copyright notice and page number") and item 5 ("script runs from CLI without
  manual intervention and overwrites previous output") could be folded into a
  single "the artifact is publication-ready and re-runnable" outcome.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces the cover page text verbatim (title, author,
  entity, copyright string, date) — the same forbidden content the outcome
  carries. Per the contract, examples should be brief and pattern-illustrative,
  not byte-faithful reproductions.
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

## Retro

This spec stayed close to brevity caps but failed the forbidden-content scan
because the spec writer treated legal-accuracy concerns as license to encode
exact strings in outcomes. The scan is the right defense — if a value's
correctness can be guaranteed by a unit test asserting a code constant, that
value belongs in code, not in the spec. The fix is structural: lift the
title/author/date triple into a module constant in the script, add a test
that asserts the cover page contains them, and let the spec commit to "the
cover matches the author's copyright record" without naming what that record
is.
